scenarios.modelAnswer:
Read the code as three independent contracts and it has broken all three.

**1. Gesture ownership.** Nothing declares `touch-action`, so on touch the browser keeps the right to pan the page and can take the gesture away from the handle mid-drag; that is the "it scrolls instead of dragging" report. `preventDefault()` inside `pointerdown` and `pointermove` is not the contract for this: ownership is decided when the gesture starts, which is why the declarative `touch-action` on the element is what works. Set `.drag-handle { touch-action: pan-y; }` if vertical page scrolling must survive and horizontal movement belongs to the drag, or `none` for a drag that owns both axes.

**2. Event targeting.** There is no capture, so once the pointer leaves the handle's bounds `pointermove` retargets to whatever is under the pointer and the handler stops running: that is "fast drags lose the element". Worse, `pointerup` is bound to the handle too, so releasing outside it never fires `up` — the drag has no ending at all in that case.

**3. Listener bookkeeping.** Nothing removes `move` on the paths that matter. `up` removes it only when `pointerup` arrives at the handle, `pointercancel` is never handled (the browser reclaiming the gesture fires `pointercancel`, not `pointerup`), and the returned cleanup removes `down` and `up` but not `move`. `down` then adds a second `pointermove` listener on the next press, so `onMove` fires twice per move and the element jumps twice as far; with a mouse, the leaked listener also makes plain hover over the handle move it — which is the "stuck until the next tap" report. There is also no `pointerId` guard, so a second finger on the same handle joins a drag that is already in progress.

A corrected version, keeping the `(clientX) => void` callback contract: end the drag in one shared path used by `pointerup` and `pointercancel`; capture on `pointerdown`; refuse a second pointer while `pointerId` is set; remove every listener in both that path and the unmount cleanup.

```ts
function useDrag(handle: HTMLElement, onMove: (x: number) => void) {
  let pointerId: number | null = null;

  const down = (e: PointerEvent) => {
    if (pointerId !== null) return;          // one pointer owns the drag
    pointerId = e.pointerId;
    handle.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);
    handle.addEventListener('pointermove', move);
    handle.addEventListener('pointerup', end);
    handle.addEventListener('pointercancel', end);
  };

  const move = (e: PointerEvent) => {
    if (e.pointerId !== pointerId) return;
    onMove(e.clientX);
  };

  const end = (e: PointerEvent) => {
    if (e.pointerId !== pointerId) return;
    if (handle.hasPointerCapture(e.pointerId)) {
      handle.releasePointerCapture(e.pointerId);
    }
    pointerId = null;
    handle.removeEventListener('pointermove', move);
    handle.removeEventListener('pointerup', end);
    handle.removeEventListener('pointercancel', end);
  };

  handle.addEventListener('pointerdown', down);
  return () => {
    handle.removeEventListener('pointerdown', down);
    handle.removeEventListener('pointermove', move);
    handle.removeEventListener('pointerup', end);
    handle.removeEventListener('pointercancel', end);
  };
}
```

With `.drag-handle { touch-action: pan-y; }` in the stylesheet, the three reported symptoms map one-to-one onto the three fixes: the page no longer steals the drag, a fast drag keeps its target and still ends, and the handle cannot accumulate listeners or stay armed after a cancelled gesture. A drag that changes a value (position, quantity, order) still needs a non-gesture path — arrow keys on a focusable handle or explicit buttons — and should be exercised with mouse, pen and touch rather than filtered by `pointerType`.

